\description{
Reports each source of a rating's uncertainty on its own, and the
combination, so a reviewer can see what a band is made of. It is not a
complete uncertainty budget, and every result says so.

Three sources can be assessed:
\itemize{
\item \strong{\code{sampling}}: the rating would differ with a different set of gaugings.
Case resampling of the gaugings used, refitting each draw with the
settings the rating recorded. An interval.
\item \strong{\code{gauging}}: each gauging is itself an estimate. Its discharge is
perturbed by its stated relative standard uncertainty, log-normally, and
the rating refitted. An interval. See \emph{Gauging error} below.
\item \strong{\code{structure}}: another reasonable fitting choice would give a different
curve: the other objective, a breakpoint moved, another valid starting
point. A \strong{range}, the lowest and highest curve across those variants
and the fitted rating. It is never an interval, because the variants
have no probability. It depends entirely on which variants were tried.
}

Where both intervals are assessed, a \code{combined} interval is also given. It
comes from joint simulation (each draw resamples the gaugings and then
perturbs them), not from adding the two.
}
\section{Gauging error}{

The package does not supply a measurement uncertainty. It takes one from the
evidence's own \code{discharge_uncertainty} column (a fraction per gauging, such
as \code{0.05}), or from \code{gauging_uncertainty} for any gauging the column leaves
blank. With neither, the gauging source is reported as \strong{not assessed}, with
the reason. It is never reported as zero.

\code{error_structure} says how the error is shared:
\itemize{
\item \code{"shared"} (default): one error per draw, common to all gaugings from the
same \code{source_name}, as when one meter or one method is biased. This is the
part of gauging error that the scatter of the points about the curve
cannot show, so it is the part that adds to the sampling interval.
\item \code{"independent"}: a separate error for each gauging. Independent error is
\strong{already in the sampling interval}, because it is part of the scatter the
